**Corollary 5.2 (bounded twelve-history example).** For the rational
preparation of Proposition 3.1 take

$$
a=1/16,\quad b=3/16,\quad d=1/32,\quad L=1,\quad
x_*=(\sqrt{21}-1)/32,\quad
\delta_*=(5-\sqrt{21})/21.
\tag{AC.13}
$$

Worst complete-output error on these twelve histories is at least
$\delta_*>0$. The scalar survival-bit task on the whole interval
$[a,b]$ attains this value with the effect

$$
E=\operatorname{diag}_{(+,-,2)}
 \left(1,\frac5{21},\frac{29-2\sqrt{21}}{21}\right).
\tag{AC.14}
$$

Proof. Substitution in (AC.11) gives $\delta_*$. All entries in (AC.14)
lie in $[0,1]$, and its probability is
$Q(x)=32x/21+(17-\sqrt{21})/21=\ell(x)+\delta_*$, independently
of $\beta,m$. Theorem 5.1 proves the matching bit lower bound.
This effect specifies only two outcome probabilities; it supplies no
correct live quantum state, exact time/mode law or lawful acquired actuator.

Every seven-unit witness has support at least $1/32$, height speed at most
$1/16$, phase speed at most $8\pi$ and phase variation at most $16\pi$.
Indeed the six prefix velocities are
$(0,-1/16,0,1/16,0,x-a_0)$ and the requested velocity is $1/32$.
The finite nuisance choices give $B\in\{0,-4\pi,8\pi,4\pi\}$.
Both regularity alternatives hold. Formula (AC.6) gives $q_x\geq2/9$
on this interval. If the original prefix is left unchanged and every old
stopped archive retained, a history attaining the conditional lower bound
has complete unconditional error at least $(2/9)\delta_*$. The prefix
errors are zero and the live-block norm is multiplied by its actual $q_x$.
$\square$

